{"ts": 1789778970.83164, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "The signal here looks genuine rather than mined. The evidence: all three components are individually strong (0.65, 0.57, 0.50), all three pairwise combinations are strong and mutually consistent (0.88, 0.82, 0.76), and the triple exceeds every pair. That coherent nesting is the signature of real, partially-independent signal \u2014 mined noise doesn't usually stack this cleanly.\n\nSizing my prediction:\n\n- **Sampling noise**: SE of an annualized Sharpe at n=5000 is \u2248 \u221a(1/5000)\u00b7\u221a252 \u2248 0.22 in annualized units. So 1.011 carries a \u00b10.22 one-sigma band on pure estimation error alone.\n- **Selection bias**: I searched ~70 specs and picked the max. But the winner wasn't a surprise \u2014 it's built from the top-3 standalone features, so the selection was mostly pre-determined by strong marginals rather than by fishing over many coin flips. I'd charge a modest haircut, ~0.10\u20130.15, not the larger penalty a blind max-of-70 would warrant.\n- **Regime/structural decay**: the real risk. Feature relationships drift out-of-sample; correlations between f24, f15 and f6 that make the combination work may weaken. This argues for both a further haircut and fatter uncertainty.\n\nCentering at roughly **0.80** and setting **sd = 0.28** to cover estimation noise plus regime drift."}
